When an element leaves “display: none”, Ladybird builds its box as a
change of its layout parent’s child list, whatever display it’s shown
with, and whether or not its own children changed while it was hidden.

Blink marks such an element with Node::SetNeedsReattachLayoutTree(), and
Element::RebuildLayoutTree() then attaches it among the layout children
of its parent. In WebKit, RenderTreeUpdater::updateElementRenderer()
calls createRenderer() for it where the update holds Style::Change::
Renderer. Gecko posts nsChangeHint_ReconstructFrame for the element,
and nsCSSFrameConstructor::RecreateFramesForContent() builds its
frames. None of the three keeps a mark of its own for children inserted
into an element without a box, so nothing there can stand in for the
mark that builds the box.

In Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0 and WebKit 26.5, the test’s
25 cases come out the same.
